Date: Thu, 11 Nov 1993 13:06:32 -0500 (EST)
From: david@bitdance.MV.COM (R. David Murray)
Subject: QUES: 'violation' vs 'misdemeanor'; trade names at common law

I'm looking for some legal info here.  If someone wants to provide it
gratis (and the first question ought to be pretty simple <grin>), great.
If someone wants to offer to sell me the info, make a bid (Thornes or $),
and I'll decide if it's worth the cost.

Question 1: Section 349:9 of the New Hampshire Revised Statutes Annotated reads
(according to the sheet that came with my trade name application)

  Penalty.  Any person who fails to comply with the provisions of the
  preceeding sections of this chapter shall be guiltly of a violation
  if a natural person, or guiltly of a misdemeanor if any other person.

Now, I imagine that 'natural person' means Citizen. But what is a 'violation',
what what penalty does it impose?  I would have thought they'd have to
spell out the fine, or something . . . 

Question 2: Section 349:11 reads:

  Prior Rights.  Nothing herein shall adversely affect the rights or the
  enforcement of rights in trade names acquired in good faith at any time
  at common law.

How does one acquire rights in trade names at common law?  I would guess:
use it first.  How does *this* relate to 349:9, given that 349:1 says "Every
sole proprietor [...] shall register the trade name of such a business"?
My guess is, this means if you don't register and do use a name, you get
it via common law, but they still fine you for not registering.  But since
it is clear I don't think like a lawyer/legislator, I'd like to check
this interpretation.  I'm especially curious about the common law provisions
for trade names.  Especially since the statute says if you are registered
you can sue someone to get them to stop using your trade name, yet if they've
acquired that trade name at common law before you've registered, it seems
349:11 means you can't, in fact, sue.  Which is fine by me, if acquiring
a trade name at common law means roughtly what I suppose it means.
Hmm, I guess that's Question 3 (can you be sued if you've acquired the
name at common law?).

-- 
david                                     (BitDance Enterprises)

