Subject: Press-Bites

Here's an opportunity for us to promote Extropian memes.  A major
British national newspaper is running a feature called "Answers To
Correspondents".  Readers are invited to write or fax in either with
a question that they want answered, or with an answer to a question
that's already appeared.  The maximum space allocated for answers
tends to be about 200 words.  Below is a question that recently
appeared, and my proposed reply of 135 words.  You'll see that I've
taken some inspiration from the Extropian Principles -- I think the
phrases are sufficiently rewritten so as not to infringe copyright.
Before I send it off, I'd like to "hear" any suggested improvements.  

Sometimes several answers are published alongside each other, but in
this case the maximum space allowed is still about 200 words.  They
may print alternative answers that come in a few days later.  

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q:  If today's animals have evolved over millions of years, how will they
change in the future?  Or has evolution stopped?

A:  Evolution is alive and well.  In the next 50 to 200 years there are
likely to be changes that dwarf those of the last few million years,
and it's the development of humans that is of most relevance to us.

    Thanks to technologies and methodologies such as genetic engineering,
artificial intelligence, fuzzy logic, neural-computer integration,
genetic algorithms, neural networks, uploading, and nanotechnology, some
of us will seize our opportunities to propel nature's evolution to 
towering new heights.  The rational exploitation of science and
technology will crush natural biological limitations, enabling us to
increase lifespan and brainpower by a millionfold and more.

    There will be two sorts of people:  Those who meekly accept "natural"
limits to lifespan, intelligence, experience and freedom, and those who
don't.  No prizes for guessing who becomes the galaxy's dominant animals. 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

If we can get a suitable answer published, the next step would be to 
have someone write in asking "How can I find out more about this?"....

Well, I can't see entropian deathist pessimists being able to come up with
anything especially exciting, more likely something similar to:
"some animals will have five legs and two tails". 

Mentioning ideas such as companion animals that have been engineered to
"want" to lick up all the dust in their master's home might have the
moralists up in arms.  And would it be better to play down increases
of "a millionfold" and substitute "several orders of magnitude"?

If anyone else wants to send in their own reply (they can just mention
that they're responding to the correspondent E.M. Clarkstone's question
as quoted above), mail to: Answers to Correspondents, Features Department,
Daily Mail, Northcliffe House, 2 Derry Street, London W8 5TT, England.
Fax number is 061-953 4050.

